\section{Built-in Sorting}

\begin{note}
In python, both \lstinline{sorted(xs)} and \lstinline{xs.sort()} accept a \lstinline{key} argument,
which is a function that determines the sort order.
\end{note}

\begin{note}
Example \lstinline{key} functions include the builtins
\lstinline{len} and \lstinline{abs}, the string methods
\lstinline{str.lower} and \lstinline{str.upper}, and functions
from the \lstinline{operator} module such as
\lstinline{operator.itemgetter} (which fetches an index) and
\lstinline{operator.attrgetter} (which fetches an attribute).
The \lstinline{lambda} keyword creates an anonymous function
in-line, which is convenient for small expressions like
\lstinline{lambda d: d['age']}. All of these are ordinary
functions, so they can be passed around, stored in variables,
or returned from other functions.
\end{note}

\begin{note}
The \lstinline{key} function is called \emph{once} per element,
so a sort using \lstinline{key=} makes $\Theta(n)$ calls to the key
function, whereas the older style of passing a comparison
function via \lstinline{functools.cmp_to_key} makes
$O(n \log n)$ calls. Both produce the same sorted order, but the
\lstinline{key} style is much faster in practice because the
expensive python-level call happens outside the inner sorting
loop.
\end{note}
